label chapter_two:

    scene bg library

    narrator "Thursday."
    narrator "You're here."
    narrator "Obviously."

    narrator "He's already there."
    narrator "He's always already there."
    narrator "You've tried leaving earlier."
    narrator "It doesn't help."

    thought "I don't say that out loud."

    orion "Potential difference."

    mc "Work done per unit charge against the field."

    orion "Why against?"

    mc "Because work done along the field is the field's work, not yours."
    mc "The sign flips. The physics changes."

    narrator "He writes something."
    narrator "You've stopped trying to read it upside down."
    narrator "You've started watching his hand instead."
    narrator "You stop doing that too."

    orion "Equipotential surfaces."

    mc "Same potential at every point. No work done moving along them."
    mc "Always perpendicular to field lines."

    orion "Why perpendicular."

    mc "Because if they weren't, there'd be a force component along the surface."
    mc "Work would be done. That contradicts the definition."

    orion "Good."

    narrator "You look up."
    narrator "He said it like it cost him nothing."

    thought "He's never said that before."
    thought "Three weeks. Not once."
    thought "I'm not going to make it mean something."
    thought "I already have."

    mc "Don't do that."

    orion "Do what."

    mc "Say 'good' like it's a reward."

    orion "I said it because it was."
    orion "Not to reward you."

    mc "There's a difference?"

    orion "Yes."

    narrator "You go back to your notes."
    narrator "The word stays in the air."
    narrator "You can feel it."
    narrator "You hate that you can feel it."

    orion "Capacitors. Teach it to me."

    mc "You know what a capacitor is."

    orion "Teach it anyway."

    thought "He does this."
    thought "Asks for things he already has."
    thought "I used to think it was arrogance."
    thought "Now I think it's something else."
    thought "I don't want to know what."

    mc "Two conducting plates. Dielectric between them."
    mc "Stores energy in the field. C equals Q over V."

    orion "Why is a farad impractically large?"

    mc "One coulomb per volt. One coulomb is already enormous."
    mc "Real circuits use microfarads. Picofarads."
    mc "We named the unit after Faraday and then immediately made it irrelevant at that scale."

    narrator "He looks up."
    narrator "Like you've said something he didn't expect."

    orion "That's the first thing you've said that wasn't from a textbook."

    mc "I say things that aren't from textbooks."

    orion "Not about Physics."

    narrator "You open your mouth."
    narrator "Close it."
    narrator "He's right."

    thought "I've been treating Physics like a language I'm learning for a visa."
    thought "Not because I want to live there."
    thought "Just to get through the border."
    thought "He lives there."
    thought "He actually lives there."
    thought "I don't know what to do with that."

    jump chapter_three
